      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** It consists of two parts hydrogen and one part oxygen.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is more \"electronegative\" (it pulls electrons toward itself) than the hydrogen atoms. This gives water a \"bent\" shape and creates a slight negative charge on one side of the molecule and a slight positive charge on the other. This polarity is what makes water such a good solvent (it can dissolve many other substances).\n\n### 2. States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist in all three states of matter at or near the temperature of the Earth's surface:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** At room temperature, water flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n### 3. Hydrogen Bonding\nBecause of its polarity, water molecules are attracted to one another. They form \"hydrogen bonds\" with each other. This is why:\n*   **Ice floats:** When water freezes, the molecules spread out, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is crucial for life, as it allows lakes to freeze from the top down, insulating the water below.\n*   **It has a high boiling point:** It takes a lot of energy to break the bonds between water molecules, which is why water holds heat well (this is why the ocean moderates the Earth's climate).\n\n### 4. The Universal Solvent\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is essential for biological processes, allowing nutrients to move through plants and animals.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a transparent, odorless, tasteless liquid that is essential for the existence of all known forms of life. It covers about 71% of the Earth's surface and makes up about 60% of the human body."
